    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Week 14 Self-Assessment\"\nsubtitle: \"Probability & Distributions\"\n---\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\n## Instructions\n\nThis quiz covers probability concepts and the normal distribution. Understanding these concepts is essential for interpreting statistical tests.\n\n---\n\n## Part 1: The Normal Distribution\n\n### Question 1: Properties of the Normal Distribution\n\nThe normal distribution is:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Skewed to the right</option><option value=''>Skewed to the left</option><option value='answer'>Symmetrical and bell-shaped</option><option value=''>Flat and uniform</option></select>\n\nIn a normal distribution, the mean, median, and mode are:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>All different values</option><option value='answer'>All the same value</option><option value=''>Mean is highest</option><option value=''>Mode is lowest</option></select>\n\n### Question 2: The 68-95-99.7 Rule\n\nIn a normal distribution:\n\nApproximately **68%** of scores fall within <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>1 standard deviation of the mean</option><option value=''>2 standard deviations of the mean</option><option value=''>3 standard deviations of the mean</option><option value=''>The interquartile range</option></select>\n\nApproximately **95%** of scores fall within <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>1 standard deviation of the mean</option><option value='answer'>2 standard deviations of the mean</option><option value=''>3 standard deviations of the mean</option><option value=''>The interquartile range</option></select>\n\nApproximately **99.7%** of scores fall within <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>1 standard deviation of the mean</option><option value=''>2 standard deviations of the mean</option><option value='answer'>3 standard deviations of the mean</option><option value=''>The interquartile range</option></select>\n\n### Question 3: Understanding Standard Deviation\n\nIf a test has M = 100 and SD = 15, a score of 115 is:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>1 SD above the mean</option><option value=''>2 SDs above the mean</option><option value=''>1 SD below the mean</option><option value=''>At the mean</option></select>\n\nA score of 70 would be:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>1 SD below the mean</option><option value='answer'>2 SDs below the mean</option><option value=''>At the mean</option><option value=''>Above average</option></select>\n\n---\n\n## Part 2: Z-Scores\n\n### Question 4: Z-Score Interpretation\n\nA z-score tells you:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>The raw score value</option><option value=''>The percentile rank</option><option value='answer'>How many standard deviations a score is from the mean</option><option value=''>Whether the score is significant</option></select>\n\nA z-score of **+2.0** means the score is:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>2 points above average</option><option value='answer'>2 standard deviations above the mean</option><option value=''>In the top 2%</option><option value=''>Twice the mean</option></select>\n\nA z-score of **-1.5** means the score is:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>1.5 points below average</option><option value='answer'>1.5 standard deviations below the mean</option><option value=''>In the bottom 1.5%</option><option value=''>Negative</option></select>\n\n### Question 5: Z-Score Calculation\n\nIf M = 50 and SD = 10, what is the z-score for a raw score of 70?\n\nz = <input class='webex-solveme nospaces ignorecase' size='4' data-answer='[\"2\",\"2.0\",\"+2\",\"+2.0\"]'/>\n\nIf M = 100 and SD = 15, what is the z-score for a raw score of 85?\n\nz = <input class='webex-solveme nospaces ignorecase' size='4' data-answer='[\"-1\",\"-1.0\"]'/>\n\n---\n\n## Part 3: Probability Basics\n\n### Question 6: Probability Values\n\nProbability values range from:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>-1 to +1</option><option value='answer'>0 to 1</option><option value=''>0 to 100</option><option value=''>-100 to +100</option></select>\n\nA probability of 0 means:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>The event is impossible</option><option value=''>The event is certain</option><option value=''>The event is likely</option><option value=''>We don't know</option></select>\n\nA probability of 1 means:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>The event is impossible</option><option value='answer'>The event is certain</option><option value=''>The event is unlikely</option><option value=''>We need more data</option></select>\n\n### Question 7: Rare Events\n\nIn statistics, we typically consider an event \"rare\" or \"unlikely\" if its probability is less than:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>0.50 (50%)</option><option value=''>0.10 (10%)</option><option value='answer'>0.05 (5%)</option><option value=''>0.01 (1%)</option></select>\n\nThis threshold is called:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>The confidence level</option><option value='answer'>The alpha level (α)</option><option value=''>The effect size</option><option value=''>The power</option></select>\n\n---\n\n## Part 4: Why Distributions Matter\n\n### Question 8: Parametric vs Non-Parametric\n\nParametric tests (like the t-test) assume:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Data are approximately normally distributed</option><option value=''>Data are skewed</option><option value=''>Sample size is very small</option><option value=''>Variables are categorical</option></select>\n\nWhen data are NOT normally distributed, we should consider using:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>A larger sample</option><option value=''>A different research design</option><option value='answer'>Non-parametric tests</option><option value=''>No statistical test</option></select>\n\n### Question 9: Assessing Normality\n\nWhich of the following helps assess whether data are normally distributed?\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Histogram and Q-Q plot</option><option value=''>Bar chart</option><option value=''>Pie chart</option><option value=''>Correlation coefficient</option></select>\n\n### Question 10: DataLab Example\n\nIf reaction times in DataLab are positively skewed (most fast, some very slow), this means:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>The distribution is normal</option><option value='answer'>The distribution has a long tail to the right</option><option value=''>The distribution has a long tail to the left</option><option value=''>We cannot analyse the data</option></select>\n\nFor skewed reaction time data, we might:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Ignore the problem</option><option value='answer'>Use a non-parametric test or transform the data</option><option value=''>Collect new data</option><option value=''>Report the median instead of mean</option></select>\n\n---\n\n## Part 5: Standard Deviation Deepened\n\n### Question 11: What SD Tells Us\n\nA larger standard deviation indicates:\n\n<select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Higher average scores</option><option value=''>Lower average scores</option><option value='answer'>More variability/spread in the data</option><option value=''>Less variability in the data</option></select>\n\nTwo groups have the same mean (M = 50). Group A has SD = 5, Group B has SD = 15.\n\nWhich group has more consistent scores? <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Group A</option><option value=''>Group B</option><option value=''>They're the same</option><option value=''>Cannot tell</option></select>\n\n### Question 12: Interpreting Spread\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nopts_sd <- c(\n  \"The data points are all exactly at the mean\",\n  answer = \"There is moderate variability — most scores are within about 10 points of the mean\",\n  \"The data are highly variable with scores spread far from the mean\",\n  \"The standard deviation is too large to interpret\"\n)\n```\n:::\n\n\nIf M = 75 and SD = 10, what does this tell us about the data?\n\n<div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_WUMUHQXBUA'><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_WUMUHQXBUA\" value=\"\"></input> <span>The data points are all exactly at the mean</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_WUMUHQXBUA\" value=\"answer\"></input> <span>There is moderate variability — most scores are within about 10 points of the mean</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_WUMUHQXBUA\" value=\"\"></input> <span>The data are highly variable with scores spread far from the mean</span></label><label><input type=\"radio\" autocomplete=\"off\" name=\"radio_WUMUHQXBUA\" value=\"\"></input> <span>The standard deviation is too large to interpret</span></label></div>\n\n\n---\n\n## Part 6: MCQ Exam Practice\n\n::: {.webex-check .webex-box}\n\n### Investigation 1\n\nA researcher measures anxiety scores in a sample of 100 students. The distribution is approximately normal with M = 50 and SD = 10.\n\n1. **What percentage of students score between 40 and 60?** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>50%</option><option value='answer'>About 68%</option><option value=''>About 95%</option><option value=''>100%</option></select>\n\n2. **A student scores 70. Their z-score is:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>0</option><option value=''>1</option><option value='answer'>2</option><option value=''>7</option></select>\n\n3. **This student's score is:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Average</option><option value=''>Slightly above average</option><option value='answer'>Well above average (top ~2.5%)</option><option value=''>Below average</option></select>\n\n4. **The data being normally distributed means we can use:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value='answer'>Parametric tests like the t-test</option><option value=''>Only non-parametric tests</option><option value=''>Only descriptive statistics</option><option value=''>No statistical tests</option></select>\n\n:::\n\n::: {.webex-check .webex-box}\n\n### Investigation 2\n\nA researcher finds that exam scores are negatively skewed (most high, few low).\n\n1. **The distribution has a long tail to the:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Right</option><option value='answer'>Left</option><option value=''>Both sides equally</option><option value=''>Neither side</option></select>\n\n2. **In this distribution, the mean is likely:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>Equal to the median</option><option value=''>Higher than the median</option><option value='answer'>Lower than the median</option><option value=''>Cannot tell</option></select>\n\n3. **A parametric test may not be appropriate because:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>The sample is too large</option><option value='answer'>The normality assumption is violated</option><option value=''>The scores are too high</option><option value=''>We need more variables</option></select>\n\n4. **An alternative approach would be to use:** <select class='webex-select'><option value='blank'></option><option value=''>A larger sample</option><option value='answer'>A non-parametric test (e.g., Mann-Whitney U)</option><option value=''>A correlation</option><option value=''>No test at all</option></select>\n\n:::\n\n---\n\n## Summary Checklist\n\nBefore moving on, make sure you can:\n\n- [ ] Describe the properties of the normal distribution\n- [ ] Apply the 68-95-99.7 rule\n- [ ] Calculate and interpret z-scores\n- [ ] Explain probability values (0 to 1)\n- [ ] Understand why α = .05 is used as a threshold\n- [ ] Distinguish parametric from non-parametric tests\n- [ ] Assess normality using histograms and Q-Q plots\n- [ ] Recognise positive and negative skew\n\n::: {.callout-important}\n## Research Plan Connection\nYour Research Plan should specify whether your data are likely to be normally distributed and justify your choice of parametric or non-parametric test accordingly.\n:::\n",
